const ENG_U4T1: SeedCard[] = [
  // simple_familiar
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'narrative perspective'.", back: "The vantage point from which a story is told — e.g. first person, third person limited, third person omniscient." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What is meant by 'authorial context' when analysing a literary text?", back: "The personal circumstances, beliefs and historical/cultural moment of the writer that shape the text's construction and meaning." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'motif'.", back: "A recurring image, phrase or idea that develops and reinforces a text's themes." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Name the technique of changing who narrates a story to reveal a different meaning.", back: "Intervening in the narrative perspective (narrative intervention)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'transformation' as applied to adapting a text into a different genre or medium.", back: "Reshaping a text's content into a new genre or medium (e.g. novel to film), which changes how meaning is made and received." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "State the difference between 'form' and 'content' in a creative text.", back: "Form is the structural/genre choices (e.g. poem, monologue, short story); content is the subject matter and ideas explored within that form." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What does 'personal style' mean for a writer producing imaginative texts?", back: "The distinctive, recognisable combination of language choices, structure and voice a writer consistently uses." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'symbolism'.", back: "Using an object, image or figure to represent a deeper idea or theme beyond its literal meaning." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Sequence the writing process for producing an imaginative text: plan → ? → ? → ?", back: "Plan → draft → edit → proofread (with reflection on feedback throughout)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'juxtaposition'.", back: "Placing two contrasting elements side by side to highlight their difference and create meaning." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "{{c1::Mood}} is the emotional atmosphere a text creates for its reader, distinct from the narrator's own tone.", back: "Mood is the emotional atmosphere a text creates for its reader, distinct from the narrator's own tone.", cardType: "cloze" },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What is it called when a writer gives a non-human thing human qualities?", back: "Personification." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'narrative voice'.", back: "The distinctive personality and style through which a story's narrator speaks, shaping how events are perceived." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What should editorial independence in drafting a creative text involve?", back: "Independently planning, drafting, editing and proofreading your own work — using strategies for revision rather than relying solely on external correction." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'genre convention' in the context of imaginative writing (e.g. for a gothic short story).", back: "An expected structural or stylistic feature of a genre (e.g. a gothic story's dark setting and sense of dread) that signals the genre to a reader." },
  // complex_familiar
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how changing a story's narrative perspective from third-person omniscient to first-person limited changes what the reader knows. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: First-person limited restricts the reader to only what that one character perceives, thinks or is told. 1 mark: This removes the omniscient narrator's access to other characters' thoughts and events happening elsewhere, creating gaps, suspense or unreliability the original didn't have." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why a writer might choose to withhold a symbol's meaning until late in an imaginative text rather than explaining it immediately. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Withholding meaning builds intrigue and rewards attentive rereading/interpretation. 1 mark: The symbol accumulates significance as it recurs, so its full meaning lands with more impact once finally clarified, rather than being flatly stated upfront." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Compare the effect of writing a scene in present tense versus past tense in an imaginative text. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Present tense creates immediacy and urgency, placing the reader inside the unfolding moment. 1 mark: Past tense creates distance and a sense of retrospection, as if events are being recounted after the fact. 1 mark: Present tense suits tense, fast-moving scenes; past tense suits reflective or reminiscent moments." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how adapting a text's setting from a historical period to a near-future setting changes what themes the transformation can foreground. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: A historical setting foregrounds themes tied to the values/constraints of that specific past era. 1 mark: A near-future setting lets the writer foreground contemporary anxieties (e.g. technology, climate) by projecting them forward. 1 mark: The underlying theme can stay the same, but the transformation changes which real-world concerns it most directly comments on." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how using short, fragmented sentences in a tense scene affects pacing and reader experience. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Short, fragmented sentences force the reader to pause frequently, slowing the reading rhythm even as events feel urgent. 1 mark: This mimics breathless, panicked thought, making the reader feel the character's tension rather than just being told about it." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "A writer wants to reveal a character's hidden bias without directly stating it. Select the more effective method — an internal monologue stating the bias, or dialogue and action that imply it — and justify your choice. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Dialogue and action that imply the bias is more effective. 1 mark: Directly stating the bias in monologue tells the reader what to conclude, removing their interpretive work. 1 mark: Implying it through dialogue/action lets the reader infer the bias themselves, which is more persuasive and more true to how bias usually surfaces in real behaviour." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how a writer's own cultural context might unconsciously shape the assumptions embedded in a character they create. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: A writer's context (upbringing, era, culture) supplies default assumptions about normal behaviour, values or speech that they may not consciously question. 1 mark: These assumptions surface in characterisation choices even when the writer isn't deliberately commenting on them, shaping the text without explicit intent." },
  // complex_unfamiliar
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "You are writing a short story that transforms a well-known fable into a modern workplace setting, keeping the same moral lesson. Devise three specific techniques you would use to signal the fable's original genre conventions to an alert reader, while the surface content is entirely modern. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: (1) Structural echo — retain the fable's compressed three-part structure (situation, test, consequence) even though events are modern (e.g. a job interview standing in for a trial), signalling the genre through shape rather than content. (2) A closing moral-statement line, stated plainly as fables traditionally do, even though nothing else in the piece is archaic — this direct address is a strong genre marker readers recognise instantly. (3) Personification or exaggerated character 'types' standing in for real animals' traditional traits (e.g. a cunning colleague behaving like the fable's fox), so the character functions the way the original animal did. Marking points: (1) proposes three genuinely distinct techniques, not variations on one idea; (2) each technique is explained with WHY it signals the genre, not just named; (3) techniques are compatible with a modern surface setting; (4) demonstrates understanding of fable as a genre with specific conventions; (5) response is specific and usable, not vague." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A student's imaginative piece about isolation is criticised by a peer for 'telling, not showing' its central emotion. Evaluate two specific rewrite strategies the student could use, and justify which better solves the actual problem described. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: Strategy A — replace a stated line such as 'she felt so alone' with concrete sensory and behavioural detail (e.g. describing the character eating dinner facing an empty chair, the silence of a phone that doesn't ring) that lets the reader infer the emotion. Strategy B — add an internal monologue elaborating on the character's loneliness in more emotive language ('the loneliness gnawed at her, hollow and endless'). Evaluation: Strategy A better solves the actual problem, because 'showing' specifically means using concrete, external detail from which the reader constructs the emotion themselves. Strategy B is still 'telling' — it is simply more elaborate telling, using more emotive vocabulary but still directly naming the feeling rather than dramatising it. Judgement: A addresses the peer's actual critique; B intensifies the original problem while appearing to fix it. Marking points: (1) proposes two distinct, genuinely different strategies; (2) explains showing vs telling accurately; (3) explains specifically why Strategy A demonstrates 'showing'; (4) explains specifically why Strategy B still 'tells'; (5) reaches and justifies a clear judgement between the two." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "You are asked to write the same short scene twice: once as a stage monologue and once as a piece of short prose fiction, both exploring a character's guilt. Analyse what a writer must change between the two versions beyond simply removing or adding stage directions. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: A monologue is performed live to a present audience by a single actor, so it must rely on rhythm, pause and vocal emphasis to convey guilt in real time — the actor's delivery does interpretive work that in prose must instead be handled by the narrator's own descriptive language. Prose fiction can access interiority directly without needing to convert every internal state into speakable dialogue, and can control pacing through paragraph structure and narrative distance rather than performance timing. The monologue's meaning is co-created with a performer and experienced communally in the moment; the prose version is experienced privately and can be reread, so its structure can plant details the reader only understands on a second pass. A writer moving between the two must convert internal states into speakable, performable language for the monologue, and convert performance cues into descriptive narration for the prose. Marking points: (1) identifies the live/performed nature of monologue vs the private/reread nature of prose; (2) explains how each mode handles interiority differently; (3) explains how pacing/rhythm is controlled differently in each mode; (4) identifies a genuine technique available to one mode but not the other; (5) explains what the writer must actively convert, not just that the modes 'are different'; (6) stays focused on guilt/interiority as the throughline." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A group of writers argues about whether a strict genre convention (e.g. the gothic novel's decaying setting) is a creative constraint that limits originality, or a tool that enables it. Justify a position, using a specific technique as evidence. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: Genre conventions are better understood as tools that enable originality rather than constraints that limit it, because a shared convention gives a writer a recognisable baseline against which departures become meaningful. For example, the gothic convention of a decaying setting mirroring psychological decline is not a limit on what a writer can say — a writer can subvert it (e.g. a bright, orderly setting concealing psychological decay) precisely because the reader's genre expectation makes the subversion legible as a deliberate choice rather than an oversight. Without the convention as a backdrop, the subversion would carry no meaning at all. The counterargument — that conventions box writers into predictable choices — has force only when a writer uses the convention unreflectively rather than as raw material to manipulate. Judgement: convention functions as an enabling tool when used deliberately, and only becomes a constraint through unoriginal, unreflective use. Marking points: (1) states a clear position; (2) gives a specific technique/example as evidence; (3) explains the mechanism by which convention enables meaningful subversion; (4) engages with the counterargument rather than ignoring it; (5) resolves with a reasoned, qualified conclusion." },
];

