// ENGLISH — Unit 4 Topic 2: Critical responses to literary texts
// ============================================================================
const ENG_U4T2: SeedCard[] = [
  // simple_familiar
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'textual integrity'.", back: "The internal consistency of a text's world, characters and ideas — the sense that its parts work together coherently to construct meaning." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'reception' as used in literary study.", back: "How a text has been read, interpreted and valued by audiences and critics — which can change across different cultural and historical contexts." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "List the aesthetic features commonly examined in a critical response to a literary text.", back: "Characterisation, plot structure, setting, narrative voice, mood, approaches to narration, imagery, symbolism, motif, figurative language, dialogue, and juxtaposition/contrast." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'narrative voice'.", back: "The distinctive perspective and personality through which a story is told, shaping how events and characters are perceived." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What is 'characterisation'?", back: "The techniques a writer uses to construct and reveal a character's personality, motivations and development." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'figurative language'.", back: "Language that departs from literal meaning to create effect — e.g. metaphor, simile, personification." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "State the difference between plot and narrative structure.", back: "Plot is the sequence of events; narrative structure is how those events are arranged and presented to the reader (e.g. non-linear, flashback, frame narrative)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'contemporaneous interpretation'.", back: "A reading or reaction to a text produced at the same time the text was originally published." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "A critic in the 1960s and a critic today interpret the same novel's ending differently. What term describes this change over time?", back: "A shift in reception (or in critical interpretation) shaped by differing cultural/historical contexts." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'cogent argument' in the context of literary analysis.", back: "A clear, logically connected argument, well supported by textual evidence, that persuades through reasoning rather than assertion." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Sequence what a literary analysis paragraph should typically contain.", back: "A topic sentence stating the point → textual evidence (quote/reference) → analysis linking the evidence to the point → a link back to the overall argument." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'setting' and its analytical function.", back: "The time and place a text occurs in — often used to construct mood, symbolism or thematic meaning, not just background detail." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "{{c1::Textual evidence}} is the quotations and specific references from a text used to substantiate a claim in a critical response.", back: "Textual evidence is the quotations and specific references from a text used to substantiate a claim in a critical response.", cardType: "cloze" },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What is it called when a narrator's account cannot be fully trusted by the reader?", back: "An unreliable narrator." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'cultural significance' of a literary text.", back: "The importance or meaning a text holds within a particular culture, often tied to what it reveals about that culture's values, history or identity." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Which cognitive verb requires drawing more than one position together and reaching a conclusion?", back: "Discuss." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'cohesive devices' in analytical writing.", back: "Words and phrases (e.g. 'furthermore', 'in contrast', 'this suggests') that connect ideas and maintain the logical flow of an argument." },
  // complex_familiar
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how narrative voice shapes a reader's trust in a first-person account of events in a novel. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: First-person narration limits the reader to one character's knowledge and biases. 1 mark: If the narrator's account contains inconsistencies or self-serving justification, the reader must independently judge how much to trust them. 1 mark: This creates a critical distance between what the narrator claims and what the text actually reveals through other cues (dialogue, other characters' reactions)." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Compare how symbolism and motif each contribute to theme development across a novel. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: A symbol represents an idea through a single object or image, often used once with concentrated meaning. 1 mark: A motif is a recurring image or phrase, building meaning cumulatively through repetition across the text. 1 mark: Symbolism gives a moment of concentrated meaning; motif builds and reinforces a theme progressively over the whole text." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why a text's contemporaneous critical reception might differ sharply from how it is read today. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Contemporaneous critics share the text's own cultural assumptions, so may not notice or question them. 1 mark: Modern readers apply present-day values, making visible what the original context normalised. 1 mark: The text itself hasn't changed — its reception changes because the interpretive context around it has." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how juxtaposing two characters' responses to the same event reveals theme in a literary text. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Placing contrasting responses side by side highlights what differs between the characters' values or circumstances. 1 mark: This difference itself becomes the vehicle for theme — the text argues something through the contrast rather than stating it directly." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "A critic notes a novel devotes twice as many pages to setting description in its final third as in its opening. Explain what this shift might suggest analytically. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: An increased focus on setting late in the text may signal that setting is being used symbolically to reflect a character's or the plot's culmination. 1 mark: The shift asks the reader to pay closer analytical attention to environment as meaning intensifies, not simply as backdrop." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "To argue how a novel's ending achieves textual integrity, should a critical response prioritise plot resolution or thematic consistency? Justify. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Thematic consistency should be prioritised. 1 mark: Textual integrity is about the text's ideas and world cohering meaningfully, not simply about loose plot threads being tied up. 1 mark: A plot can resolve neatly while betraying the themes established earlier, which would undermine integrity even with a 'complete' plot." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how dialogue can be used to reveal a power imbalance between two characters without the text stating it directly. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Who interrupts, who asks questions versus gives orders, and sentence length/formality can all encode status differences. 1 mark: A reader infers the power imbalance from these patterns rather than from explicit narration. 1 mark: This makes the imbalance feel embedded in the world of the text rather than imposed by an external narrator's judgement." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why analysing a text's cultural significance requires more than summarising its plot. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Cultural significance concerns what the text reveals about or contributes to a culture's values, history or identity — not what happens in the story. 1 mark: A plot summary describes events; establishing significance requires connecting those events to the broader context the text emerged from and speaks to." },
  // complex_unfamiliar
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A novel praised on publication for its 'realistic' portrayal of a marginalised community is later criticised by members of that community for relying on stereotype. Analyse how this shift in reception reflects a change in whose perspective is treated as authoritative when judging textual integrity. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: On publication, 'realistic' was judged by an audience/critical establishment external to the community depicted, whose own assumptions determined what counted as authentic — the novel could appear internally consistent and convincing to readers with no independent standard to check it against. The later criticism from within the community introduces a different standard of authority: lived experience as evidence, which exposes gaps between the text's constructed 'realism' and actual complexity, revealing the earlier praise as itself shaped by the limited perspective of its original audience. This does not necessarily mean the text's textual integrity has changed — the novel is unchanged — but the standard against which coherence and authenticity are measured has shifted: what once passed as consistent world-building can later read as consistent stereotype. Marking points: (1) identifies that the original 'realistic' judgement came from an external/non-community audience; (2) explains why that audience lacked an independent check on accuracy; (3) explains the significance of the later community-based critique as a shift in authority; (4) distinguishes between the text's unchanged textual integrity and its changed reception; (5) makes an explicit claim about whose perspective is treated as authoritative, and why this shifted; (6) avoids treating the shift as simply 'the novel got worse'." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A critic argues that a novel's fragmented, non-linear narrative structure is 'confusing' and therefore a flaw. Evaluate this claim by considering what the fragmentation might be doing thematically. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: The claim treats confusion as an unintended failure, but fragmentation is frequently a deliberate structural choice mirroring the theme being explored — e.g. if the novel concerns trauma or memory, a disordered structure can enact the way traumatic memory itself resists linear recall, making the reader experience something structurally analogous to the character's psychological state rather than simply being told about it. Judging this as a 'flaw' assumes linear coherence is always the goal of narrative structure, which is not a neutral standard — it privileges conventional realist storytelling over other legitimate modes. That said, the claim isn't baseless if the fragmentation genuinely obscures meaning without any compensating thematic payoff. Judgement: the claim is only valid if the fragmentation fails to connect to theme; where it does connect, 'confusing' describes an intended effect, not a flaw. Marking points: (1) explains that fragmentation is often deliberate, not accidental; (2) connects the structural choice to a plausible thematic function; (3) critiques the assumption that linear coherence is the only valid standard; (4) acknowledges a genuine condition under which the critic's claim would hold; (5) reaches a qualified, reasoned judgement; (6) uses precise terminology (narrative structure, thematic function)." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "Two critical essays reach opposite conclusions about whether a novel's ambiguous ending represents hope or despair, both citing the same final image. Devise a method for adjudicating between the two readings using textual evidence beyond the final image alone. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: Since the final image alone supports both readings, adjudication must draw on evidence from earlier in the text that establishes a pattern the ending either continues or breaks. Method: (1) Trace the novel's use of the same symbol or motif earlier — if it has consistently carried negative connotations throughout, its recurrence at the end weights the reading toward despair, and vice versa. (2) Examine the narrative voice's tonal shifts leading into the ending — a narrator who has grown more resigned, or more resolved, colours how the final image should be read even if the image itself is neutral. (3) Consider the fate/arc of secondary characters resolved just before the ending, since endings rarely occur in isolation. (4) Weigh authorial pattern across the whole text as corroborating, not decisive, evidence. A rigorous critical response should show its working using at least two of these methods, since relying on the final image alone cannot resolve the ambiguity the critics are already disagreeing about. Marking points: (1) recognises that the final image alone cannot adjudicate; (2) proposes at least three genuinely distinct methods of drawing on earlier textual evidence; (3) explains the reasoning behind each method, not just naming it; (4) method is general/transferable; (5) acknowledges pattern-based evidence is corroborating rather than absolute proof; (6) demonstrates understanding that critical adjudication is evidence-based, not a matter of preference." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A school decides to teach a canonical literary text alongside a contemporary text by a marginalised author responding directly to it. Justify how studying textual integrity across both texts together might produce insights that studying either text alone would not. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: Studying the canonical text's textual integrity in isolation risks treating its internal coherence and assumptions as neutral or universal, since nothing in the syllabus context challenges them. Placing the contemporary response text alongside it makes visible which of the canonical text's internal 'coherences' actually depended on excluding or simplifying a particular perspective — the response text's own textual integrity is partly built by exposing and reworking exactly those gaps. Studying them together therefore reveals textual integrity as constructed and contestable rather than fixed: a text can be internally coherent and still rest on assumptions a later text is coherent in rejecting. Neither text alone teaches this — the canonical text alone would appear self-sufficiently coherent, and the response text alone would lose the specific target of its critique. Marking points: (1) explains the risk of studying the canonical text's coherence in isolation; (2) explains how the contemporary text exposes assumptions in the canonical one; (3) makes the explicit claim that textual integrity is revealed as constructed/contestable through the pairing; (4) explains what is specifically lost by studying either text alone; (5) reasoning is grounded in the concept of textual integrity; (6) sustains a coherent argument across the whole response." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A critic claims a text's cultural significance is fixed at the moment of publication and cannot meaningfully change afterward. Evaluate this claim, using the concept of reception to support your position. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: This claim does not hold up under the concept of reception, which shows that a text's significance is actively remade by each new context of reading, not sealed at publication. A text's cultural significance depends on what it is understood to reveal about or contribute to a culture — and as the surrounding culture's values, concerns and knowledge change, the same textual content can come to reveal different things (e.g. a novel originally read as a domestic drama can later be read as a critique of gendered labour once that framework becomes culturally available). This is not the text changing but the interpretive resources available to readers changing, which is precisely how reception theory explains shifts in a text's standing over time. The claim would only hold if 'significance' meant something narrower, like the specific facts an author intended to convey — but cultural significance, as generally used in literary study, is explicitly about ongoing cultural relevance, which is not fixed. Judgement: the claim is not well supported; significance is dynamic, actively constructed through reception across time. Marking points: (1) states a clear judgement rejecting the claim; (2) explains the mechanism — changing cultural context enables new readings without the text changing; (3) gives or implies a concrete illustrative mechanism; (4) connects the argument explicitly to the concept of reception; (5) considers and addresses a narrower sense in which the claim might be limitedly true; (6) reaches a clear, well-supported final position." },
];

// ============================================================================
// PHYSICAL EDUCATION — Unit 3 Topic 1: Tactical awareness in physical activity
// ============================================================================
const PE_U3T1: SeedCard[] = [
  // simple_familiar
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define tactical awareness.", back: "A personal response to the interaction of constraints of the learner, task and environment during goal-directed behaviour in physical activity." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Name the two major approaches to investigating motor learning.", back: "The cognitive systems approach (cognitive model) and the dynamic systems approach (ecological model)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define the cognitive systems approach to motor learning.", back: "A hierarchical model of control where higher control centres pass commands to lower control centres, producing linear changes in movement." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define the dynamic systems approach to motor learning.", back: "A model where movements emerge or self-organise through the dynamic interaction of the environment, the task and the individual — non-hierarchical and non-linear." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "List the three categories of constraints in the dynamic systems approach.", back: "Task constraints, individual (learner) constraints, and environmental constraints." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Give two examples of task constraints.", back: "Number of players and rules (also equipment)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Give two examples of individual (learner) constraints.", back: "Height/weight/body composition and motor skills (also motivation)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Give two examples of environmental constraints.", back: "Playing surface and weather conditions (also noise, peers, coach)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define perception–action coupling.", back: "A direct link between interpreting information from the environment and taking a specific action — perception drives action, and action can drive perception." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'affordance'.", back: "An opportunity for action provided by the environment or task in relation to the learner's ability, e.g. a gap between defenders affording a run." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define a constraints-led approach to learning.", back: "A teaching methodology combining dynamic systems theory (constraints on the individual) with the ecological model (interaction with the environment)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Sequence the principles of decision-making in physical activity.", back: "Reading play → recognising information and responding → reacting to implement movement → recovering with appropriate movements." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "List the four principles of play.", back: "Setting up attack, defending against attack, creating/defending/exploiting space, and attacking opposition space and scoring." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'on-the-ball' movement.", back: "Movement and decision-making performed by the player currently in possession of, or contesting, the ball." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'off-the-ball' movement.", back: "Movement and decision-making performed by a player not currently in possession of the ball, e.g. creating space or supporting." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "List the four body and movement concepts used to analyse on-the-ball and off-the-ball movement.", back: "Body awareness, space awareness, quality of movement, and relationships." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Give an example of a 'space awareness' consideration in physical activity.", back: "Movement pathways, use of space, or when to run into space versus when to pass." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'primary data' in a physical activity investigation context.", back: "Data an individual personally collects or gathers, e.g. through their own performance or observation." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'secondary data' in a physical activity investigation context.", back: "Data sourced from existing external sources rather than personally collected." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "A player spots a gap between two defenders and runs through it. Which concept from the ecological model does this best illustrate?", back: "Affordance — the gap is an opportunity for action the environment provides, which the player perceives and acts on." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "{{c1::Self-organisation}} is when a learner's movement system settles into a specific, stable pattern in response to the constraints placed upon it.", back: "Self-organisation is when a learner's movement system settles into a specific, stable pattern in response to the constraints placed upon it.", cardType: "cloze" },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Are changes in movement under dynamic systems theory linear or non-linear?", back: "Non-linear — they involve abrupt changes from one stable state to another (e.g. walking to running as treadmill speed increases)." },
  // complex_familiar
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why the cognitive systems approach struggles to fully explain decision-making in fast, unpredictable game play. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: The cognitive approach assumes a hierarchical process — perceiving, deciding, then executing in sequence. 1 mark: Authentic game play is complex and rapidly changing, leaving little time for this linear sequence to complete before the situation changes. 1 mark: The dynamic systems approach better explains this because it treats movement as emerging directly from perception–action coupling, without a slow hierarchical decision chain." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how manipulating a task constraint (e.g. reducing the playing area) can develop a specific tactical skill. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: A smaller playing area increases the density of players relative to space, forcing more frequent decision-making. 1 mark: This compresses the time available to read play and react, developing faster perception–action coupling. 1 mark: Because the constraint — not direct instruction — drives the adaptation, the resulting skill emerges as a stable, self-organised response suited to tight-space play." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why individual (learner) constraints mean the same task constraint can produce different tactical outcomes for two different players. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Each learner brings different individual constraints (skill level, physical attributes, motivation) to the same task. 1 mark: Because movement self-organises from the interaction of all three constraint categories, differing individual constraints change what solution emerges even under an identical task constraint." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Compare 'reading play' and 'recovering' as principles of decision-making in terms of when they occur in a possession sequence. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Reading play happens first — gathering and interpreting information before acting. 1 mark: Recovering happens last — repositioning with appropriate movement after the play has developed, to prepare for the next phase." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how off-the-ball movement can create an affordance for a teammate on the ball. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: A player moving into space without the ball changes the environmental/task configuration the on-ball player perceives. 1 mark: This creates a new affordance — an opportunity for action (e.g. a passing lane) that did not previously exist. 1 mark: The on-ball player's action (the pass) emerges directly from perceiving this newly created opportunity, illustrating perception–action coupling between teammates." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "A player's primary data shows 80% of successful attacking plays followed a period of exploiting space rather than direct confrontation. Explain what this suggests about tactical strategy. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: The data suggests exploiting space is a more effective principle of play for this player/team than direct confrontation. 1 mark: A tactical strategy built around identifying and moving into space would likely improve attacking success rates further, based on this evidence." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why analysing 'quality of movement' (e.g. force development, efficiency) matters as much as analysing the decision itself when evaluating tactical performance. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: A correct tactical decision can still fail in execution if the movement quality is poor. 1 mark: Evaluating both together distinguishes a tactical error from an execution error, which require different strategies to fix." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "To develop a player's ability to exploit space under pressure, select whether you would manipulate task constraints or individual constraints, and why. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Task constraints should be manipulated (e.g. reducing space, adding defenders). 1 mark: Individual constraints (height, strength) are largely fixed and not something training directly changes in the short term. 1 mark: Manipulating task constraints creates the specific pressure situation needed for the tactical skill to self-organise and be practised repeatedly." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain the relationship expressed in 'perception can drive the action, but action can also drive the perception.' (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Perception driving action means noticing an affordance (e.g. a gap) leads to a movement response (running through it). 1 mark: Action driving perception means that as a player moves, their changing position reveals new information that wasn't visible before. 1 mark: This means perception and action are continuously cycling and updating each other, not a single one-way process." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why using secondary data (e.g. published match statistics) alongside primary data (personal performance data) strengthens the justification of a tactical strategy. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Secondary data provides a broader evidence base beyond one individual's limited sample. 1 mark: Combining it with primary data lets a strategy be justified both by personal relevance (primary) and by wider validity (secondary)." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how the constraints-led approach differs from traditional, instruction-heavy coaching in how it develops tactical awareness. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Traditional instruction-heavy coaching tells the player what to do, often outside the pressure of real game constraints. 1 mark: The constraints-led approach instead manipulates task, individual and environmental constraints so the appropriate movement solution emerges/self-organises through practice. 1 mark: This produces tactical awareness that is adaptable to real, changing game conditions, rather than a memorised response to instruction." },
  // complex_unfamiliar
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A touch football coach notices her team performs well in structured drills but freezes under pressure in real matches. Using the dynamic systems approach, analyse why this gap between drill performance and match performance occurs, and devise one training change to address it. (7 marks)", back: "Model response: Structured drills typically remove or fix many of the constraints present in a real match — reduced defenders, predictable patterns, ample time — so players can succeed using a rehearsed, cognitively-planned response rather than genuine perception–action coupling. In a real match, task, environmental and time constraints combine to create a far more complex, self-organising demand than the drill ever presented. Because the players' movement solutions were developed under low-constraint conditions, they haven't built the perception–action coupling needed to read authentic, chaotic play, so under real pressure their decision-making reverts to hesitant, cognitively-driven processing (freezing) rather than fluid, self-organised response. Training change: replace some structured drills with small-sided, constraints-led games that manipulate task constraints (e.g. reduced space, added defenders, time pressure) to force players to develop tactical awareness under conditions that resemble the affordances and pressure of a real match. Marking points: (1) explains that drills reduce/remove authentic constraints; (2) explains that match conditions combine multiple constraint categories in complex, unpredictable ways; (3) connects the freezing behaviour to reliance on rehearsed/cognitive response rather than self-organised perception–action coupling; (4) proposes a specific, plausible training change; (5) explains why the proposed change would build transferable tactical awareness; (6) uses dynamic systems terminology accurately throughout; (7) response is grounded in the specific scenario." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "Two players have identical technical skill levels, but Player A consistently exploits space better than Player B in match play. Justify, using the concepts of affordance and individual constraints, what might explain this difference despite equal technical ability. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: Since technical skill is equal, the difference likely lies in perceptual attunement — the ability to notice affordances rather than in the physical execution of movement. Individual constraints beyond raw technical skill — such as experience, attentional focus, or motivation to actively scan for space — shape how attuned a player has become to the affordances available in a given environment; Player A may simply have developed a more refined perception of which environmental cues signal an exploitable gap. This is consistent with the principle that as a skill is learned, individuals become more attuned to the environment and the affordances available for movement — meaning 'skill' in tactical awareness includes this perceptual attunement, which can develop unevenly even between players of equal technical ability. Judgement: the gap is best explained by differing individual constraints related to perceptual attunement to affordances, not by technical skill, which the scenario states is equal. Marking points: (1) correctly identifies that the difference cannot be technical, since that's held equal; (2) explains affordance and its role in exploiting space; (3) connects perceptual attunement to individual constraints beyond raw technical skill; (4) explains how attunement to affordances develops with experience; (5) reaches a clear, justified conclusion tied to the specific scenario; (6) uses key terminology precisely." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A basketball team's coach wants to improve players' off-the-ball movement to create more attacking affordances for the ball carrier, but has only 20 minutes of training time per session to dedicate to this. Devise a constraints-led training activity that targets this specific goal, and justify each design choice against the principles of play. (7 marks)", back: "Model response: Activity — a small-sided game (e.g. 4v4) played in a reduced-width area, where a point is awarded not for scoring but for a pass received by a teammate who moved into space to receive it, forcing players off the ball to actively create affordances rather than stand still. Design justifications: (1) The reduced area (task constraint) increases defensive density, meaning space has to be actively created rather than naturally existing, directly targeting the 'creating space' principle of play. (2) The scoring rule change makes off-the-ball movement the explicit goal of the game itself, ensuring the constraint is what drives the desired behaviour to self-organise, in line with a genuine constraints-led approach rather than direct instruction. (3) Small-sided (4v4) format increases each player's involvement frequency within the 20-minute window, maximising repetitions of the specific decision within limited time. (4) Because affordances are perceived relative to defender positioning, this format continuously varies defensive shapes, giving players repeated exposure to recognising and exploiting different space-creating opportunities. Marking points: (1) proposes a specific, plausible activity; (2) explicitly uses constraint manipulation rather than direct instruction; (3) links the design to the 'creating space' principle of play; (4) explains how the time-limited format is addressed; (5) explains why constraint-driven learning is the more effective mechanism here; (6) connects the activity back to affordance for the ball carrier specifically; (7) response is tailored to the scenario's stated constraint." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A netball player performs excellently in training (structured, predictable drills) but her decision-making accuracy drops significantly in unpredictable, game-like scenarios in the same training session. Analyse this using the concepts of constraints and self-organisation, and evaluate whether more repetition of the same drill would fix the problem. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: The drop in decision-making accuracy under unpredictable conditions suggests her movement solutions were self-organised specifically around the fixed, predictable constraints of the structured drill. When environmental and task constraints become unpredictable, the previously stable solution no longer fits the new configuration of constraints, and because dynamic systems theory holds that movement emerges from the specific interaction of constraints present, a solution tuned to one constraint set does not automatically transfer to another. Evaluation of more repetition: simply repeating the same predictable drill would not fix the problem, because it would only reinforce movement solutions suited to low-variability conditions, not build the perceptual attunement and adaptable self-organisation needed for unpredictable play. What is needed instead is variation in the constraints presented during practice, so a broader, more adaptable range of movement solutions can self-organise. Judgement: more repetition of the same drill would not solve the issue; varying the constraints during practice would. Marking points: (1) explains that the drill's fixed constraints produced a narrowly-tuned solution; (2) explains why an unpredictable constraint configuration doesn't match the trained solution; (3) explicitly evaluates the 'more repetition' proposal and rejects it with reasoning; (4) proposes what would actually help; (5) uses self-organisation/constraints terminology correctly; (6) reasoning is specific to the scenario." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A junior coach believes tactical awareness cannot be taught, only 'natural talent'. Evaluate this belief against the constraints-led approach and the concept of self-organisation. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: This belief does not hold up well against the constraints-led approach, which is built on the premise that tactical awareness — a personal response to constraints during goal-directed behaviour — emerges through a learner's movement system self-organising in response to constraints that are deliberately manipulated in training. If tactical awareness were purely natural talent, manipulating task, individual and environmental constraints in training would have no effect on players' decision-making — yet self-organisation theory and the constraints-led approach are premised on exactly the opposite: that appropriately designed constraints reliably shape the emergence of tactical solutions in learners regardless of starting point. This does not mean all players will reach the same level — individual constraints genuinely vary between learners and will influence how quickly or fully tactical awareness develops — but this is a difference in the rate/ceiling of development, not evidence that development cannot be taught at all. Judgement: the coach's belief is not well supported; tactical awareness can be deliberately developed through constraint manipulation, though individual constraints will affect how readily it develops for each player. Marking points: (1) states a clear judgement on the coach's belief; (2) explains the constraints-led approach's premise; (3) explains self-organisation as the mechanism; (4) acknowledges individual constraints genuinely affect development rate; (5) distinguishes 'affects development' from 'cannot be developed'; (6) reaches a clear, reasoned final position." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A team's game analysis (secondary data) shows opponents consistently struggle against high-tempo, direct attacking play, but the team's own primary data shows their players perform tactical decision-making more accurately at a slower tempo. Justify a tactical strategy that reconciles this apparent conflict. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: The apparent conflict can be reconciled by recognising that tactical strategy should optimise for the actual outcome, not simply for what is most comfortable for the players' current decision-making accuracy in isolation. A strategy of manipulating tempo progressively — starting possessions at a controlled tempo where the team's decision-making accuracy is highest (leveraging primary data strength) and increasing tempo specifically in the final third or key moments where the opponent's known weakness (secondary data) can be exploited — uses each data source for what it is best suited to. This is preferable to committing fully to high tempo (risking the team's own weaker decision-making) or fully to low tempo (ignoring a clear opponent vulnerability). Judgement: a hybrid, situational tempo strategy — controlled build-up, targeted tempo increase to exploit the opponent's specific weakness — best reconciles the two data sources. Marking points: (1) accurately restates the apparent conflict between the two data sources; (2) proposes a specific reconciling strategy, not just picking one data source over the other; (3) explains how primary data is used within the strategy; (4) explains how secondary data is used within the strategy; (5) justifies why a hybrid approach outperforms committing to either extreme; (6) response is grounded in the specific scenario details given." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "During a match, a player successfully exploits space early in the first half but is repeatedly shut down using the same movement pattern for the rest of the game. Analyse why this occurred using the concept of the environment as an interacting, adaptive constraint, and devise one in-game adjustment the player could make. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: Environmental (and specifically opposition-related) constraints are not static — the opposition is itself an adaptive part of the system, and once defenders perceive the pattern of the player's successful movement, their positioning changes to close off that specific affordance. Because tactical awareness emerges from the interaction of constraints, a movement solution that worked under the earlier constraint configuration no longer produces the same affordance once the defensive configuration has adapted. In-game adjustment: the player should vary their off-the-ball movement patterns and timing so defenders cannot reliably anticipate and close the previously exploited space, forcing a new interaction of constraints that can generate fresh affordances. Marking points: (1) explains that the opposition/environment is an adaptive, not fixed, constraint; (2) explains why a previously successful solution stops working once the constraint configuration changes; (3) connects this to the general principle that tactical solutions emerge from the current constraint interaction; (4) proposes a specific, plausible adjustment; (5) explains why the adjustment would restore effectiveness; (6) response is grounded in the specific scenario." },
];

// ============================================================================
// PHYSICAL EDUCATION — Unit 3 Topic 2: Ethics and integrity in physical activity
// ============================================================================
const PE_U3T2: SeedCard[] = [
  // simple_familiar
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define ethics.", back: "The set of norms and ways of life through which we realise acceptable behaviour and values of right and wrong." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define integrity in the context of physical activity.", back: "The demonstration of ethics and values that promote community confidence in physical activity, including fair and honest performance and positive stakeholder engagement." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define fair play.", back: "Observing rules and demonstrating attitudes/behaviours consistent with the belief that physical activity is an ethical pursuit, including respect, team spirit and eliminating exploitation." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "List four ethical dilemmas commonly arising in physical activity contexts.", back: "Gender inclusion or exclusion, ability, enhancements in technology and equipment, and corruption." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Name the groups that influence the development of personal values and ethical behaviours in physical activity.", back: "Peers, family, coaches, school and community." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Sequence the steps of the ethical decision-making framework.", back: "1. Identify the ethical dilemma. 2. Find information (facts, stakeholders, prior strategies). 3. Evaluate alternatives. 4. Devise strategies. 5. Reflect on the outcome." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define an 'ethics strategy'.", back: "A course of action devised in response to an ethical dilemma, developed to improve the integrity of a player or organisation." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Give an example of a form of exploitation that fair play seeks to eliminate.", back: "Acts of violence, cheating, or drug abuse (doping)." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'stakeholder' in the context of ethics in physical activity.", back: "An individual or group with an important stake in the outcome of an ethical dilemma or decision, e.g. athletes, administrators, officials, supporters." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "{{c1::Corruption}} is a form of ethical dilemma in physical activity involving dishonest or fraudulent conduct for personal or organisational gain.", back: "Corruption is a form of ethical dilemma in physical activity involving dishonest or fraudulent conduct for personal or organisational gain.", cardType: "cloze" },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "State the difference between 'unwritten rules' and 'written rules' in fair play.", back: "Written rules are the formal, codified regulations of the activity; unwritten rules are informal conventions of respect, solidarity and sportsmanship not formally codified but still expected." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'globalisation' as it relates to ethics in physical activity.", back: "The increasing interconnectedness of sport worldwide, which — along with media coverage — has influenced and reshaped ethical values and behaviours." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Name the first step of the ethical decision-making framework.", back: "Identify the ethical dilemma — the problem or situation and the tension between values." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What must you find information about in step 2 of the ethical decision-making framework?", back: "The relevant facts of the problem, individuals/groups with a stake in the outcome, and strategies used in response to similar problems." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "List the criteria used when evaluating alternatives in the ethical decision-making framework.", back: "Which strategy produces the most good and least harm; best respects the rights of stakeholders; treats people equally or proportionately; best serves the community; leads players to act with integrity." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define a 'code of behaviour and conduct' in physical activity.", back: "A set of rules and policies (including risk assessment) at class, school or community level that support ethical behaviour and fair play." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "A new wearable device gives an athlete real-time biomechanical feedback banned in competition rules. What type of ethical dilemma does this represent?", back: "An enhancement in technology and equipment dilemma." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'respect' as an element of fair play.", back: "Demonstrating consideration for opponents, officials, teammates and the rules of the activity." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What must an ethics strategy's final step (reflection) determine?", back: "The effectiveness of the ethics strategy on all stakeholders." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "What term describes primary and secondary data used together to identify who has a stake in an ethical dilemma and what strategies have addressed similar problems?", back: "Analysing and synthesising primary and secondary data." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'primary data' as it applies to studying ethical dilemmas in physical activity.", back: "Data personally gathered about the relationship between ethical dilemmas, ethics concepts/principles, and engagement in physical activity." },
  { complexity: "simple_familiar", front: "Define 'secondary data' as it applies to studying ethics strategies.", back: "Existing external data used to analyse how the development of ethics strategies can influence engagement in physical activity." },
  // complex_familiar
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how media coverage can amplify an ethical dilemma around gender inclusion in physical activity. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Media coverage increases the visibility and public scrutiny of gender-inclusion decisions made by sporting bodies. 1 mark: This can pressure organisations to justify policies publicly, accelerating debate and policy change. 1 mark: It can also polarise public opinion, making the underlying ethical tension harder to resolve through calm, considered deliberation." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Compare fair play and integrity as concepts in physical activity. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Fair play concerns observable behaviours and attitudes during participation, such as following rules and respecting opponents. 1 mark: Integrity is the broader system of values and character underpinning consistent ethical behaviour and community confidence. 1 mark: Fair play is one visible expression of integrity, but integrity encompasses conduct beyond the field of play (e.g. honesty in administration)." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why the same ethics strategy might succeed in a school context but fail in a professional physical activity context. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Ethical decision-making frameworks consider the specific stakeholders and community context, which differ significantly between school and professional settings. 1 mark: A school context may prioritise participation and development, while a professional context involves financial and reputational stakes for more stakeholders. 1 mark: A strategy suited to one set of stakeholder priorities may not 'produce the most good and least harm' when applied to a different set of stakeholders." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how role models (e.g. coaches) shape an individual's ethical behaviour in physical activity. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Individuals observe and internalise the values and conduct demonstrated by influential figures like coaches. 1 mark: Consistent modelling of ethical or unethical behaviour by these role models shapes what the individual comes to see as normal or acceptable conduct." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why devising an ethics strategy requires identifying stakeholders before proposing a course of action. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Different stakeholders can be affected differently by any proposed course of action. 1 mark: Identifying them first ensures the strategy can be evaluated against whose rights it respects and who it serves, rather than being devised blind to its actual impact." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how technological enhancement in equipment can create tension between the values of fair competition and innovation. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Fair competition assumes athletes compete on comparable terms, using broadly equivalent equipment. 1 mark: Technological innovation can give an advantage to those with access to it, undermining that comparability. 1 mark: This creates an ethical tension because innovation is generally valued, but its uneven access can compromise the integrity of fair and honest outcomes." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Secondary data shows that ethics strategies involving open stakeholder consultation resolve dilemmas with fewer repeat incidents than strategies imposed without consultation. Explain what this suggests about designing an effective ethics strategy. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: The data suggests stakeholder consultation is associated with more durable, effective resolution of ethical dilemmas. 1 mark: An effective ethics strategy should therefore actively involve the relevant stakeholders in its design, not just its implementation." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how a code of behaviour can support fair play without being able to guarantee it. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: A code of behaviour sets out expected standards, giving participants a clear reference for acceptable conduct. 1 mark: It cannot guarantee fair play because compliance still depends on individuals' personal values and choices, which the code alone cannot enforce or control internally." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "When evaluating alternative responses to a corruption dilemma, select whether 'which strategy produces the most good and least harm' or 'which strategy is easiest to implement' is the more appropriate criterion from the ethical decision-making framework, and justify. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: 'Most good and least harm' is the appropriate framework criterion. 1 mark: Ease of implementation is not one of the framework's stated evaluation criteria — using it instead would prioritise convenience over the ethical outcome for stakeholders." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain the relationship between globalisation and the spread of doping-related ethical dilemmas in sport. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Globalisation increases interconnectedness between competitive systems, athletes and governing bodies across countries. 1 mark: This can spread both performance-enhancement practices and inconsistent regulation/enforcement standards across borders. 1 mark: The result is an uneven ethical landscape where doping dilemmas in one country/context can affect the perceived integrity of international competition as a whole." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain why reflecting on the outcome is a necessary final step of the ethical decision-making framework, rather than the process ending once a strategy is implemented. (2 marks)", back: "1 mark: Implementing a strategy does not guarantee it achieved the intended ethical outcome for all stakeholders. 1 mark: Reflection determines the strategy's actual effectiveness, informing whether it should be maintained or modified — without it, an ineffective or harmful strategy could continue unchecked." },
  // complex_unfamiliar
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A school athletics carnival introduces a new mixed-ability race category, but some parents complain it 'waters down' competitive integrity, while others argue excluding students with disabilities from mainstream races was always the greater ethical failure. Apply the ethical decision-making framework to this dilemma and justify a resolution. (7 marks)", back: "Model response: Step 1 (identify the dilemma) — the tension is between preserving perceived competitive integrity of mainstream races and ensuring inclusive participation for students of all abilities. Step 2 (find information) — relevant facts include participation rates before/after the change, stakeholder groups (students with disabilities and their families, competitive-focused students/parents, school administration, officials), and how other organisations have addressed similar tensions (e.g. classification systems). Step 3 (evaluate alternatives) — a strategy must be judged on which produces the most good and least harm, respects the rights of all stakeholders equally, and best serves the whole school community, not just the loudest complainants. Excluding students with disabilities fails these criteria; a mixed-ability category that maintains genuine competitive structure within it (e.g. classification-based grouping) can serve both fair competition and inclusion. Step 4 (devise a strategy) — retain the mixed-ability category but introduce classification or graded groupings within it so competitive integrity is preserved within appropriately matched groups, alongside clear communication to the school community about why the change was made. Step 5 (reflect) — the school should track participation and complaint data after implementation to evaluate whether the strategy achieved integrity and inclusion for all stakeholders. Marking points: (1) applies all five framework steps in order; (2) correctly identifies the core value tension; (3) identifies relevant stakeholders and information sources; (4) applies the framework's actual evaluation criteria; (5) proposes a specific, justified strategy grounded in the framework's criteria; (6) includes a reflection/evaluation step; (7) reaches and justifies a clear resolution rather than simply describing both sides." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A junior football club's most talented player is revealed to have used performance-enhancing supplements banned at open level but not explicitly banned in junior competition rules, which are silent on the issue. Evaluate whether this constitutes an ethical breach of integrity, even though no written rule was broken. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: This does constitute an ethical breach of integrity, even absent a specific written rule, because integrity is defined by the demonstration of ethics and values promoting community confidence — not solely by compliance with codified rules. Fair play explicitly includes eliminating forms of exploitation in an effort to win (such as drug use) as part of an ethical pursuit, independent of whether a specific rule text names the exact substance used at the junior level; the silence of the rules reflects an oversight or gap, not an endorsement. Using a substance banned at the level the player is progressing toward, specifically to gain an advantage now, undermines fair and honest performance outcomes and the trust of other stakeholders that outcomes reflect genuine ability — the core concern integrity protects. A counterargument — that without a rule, no 'breach' has technically occurred — mistakes legality for ethics; codes of conduct and rules are described as supporting ethical behaviour, not defining its full extent. Judgement: this is an ethical breach of integrity despite the rules gap, because integrity is judged against values and community confidence, not merely rule compliance. Marking points: (1) states a clear judgement; (2) explains integrity as broader than rule compliance; (3) explicitly connects to fair play's exploitation-elimination principle; (4) addresses the counterargument directly; (5) explains the impact on stakeholder trust/community confidence; (6) reaches a well-reasoned, supported conclusion." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "An amateur cycling event allows the use of a new type of bike frame that is legal but affordable only to well-funded teams, while grassroots riders cannot access it. Devise an ethics strategy the event organisers could implement, and justify each element against the ethical decision-making framework's evaluation criteria. (7 marks)", back: "Model response: Strategy — introduce an equipment-tiered category system (e.g. an 'open technology' division alongside a 'standard equipment' division using a defined baseline specification), alongside a transparency requirement that all competitors disclose equipment used, and a review period to monitor participation impacts. Justification against the framework's criteria: (1) 'Produces the most good and least harm' — separating categories prevents well-funded teams' technological advantage from determining outcomes in the standard division, while still allowing innovation in the open division. (2) 'Respects the rights of all stakeholders' — well-funded teams retain the right to use legal equipment; grassroots riders retain the right to competitive integrity within their own division. (3) 'Treats people equally or proportionately' — the tiered system treats riders proportionately to their actual resource access rather than requiring identical treatment regardless of unequal starting resources. (4) 'Best serves the community as a whole' — maintaining broad participation across both grassroots and well-funded riders serves the overall health and growth of the sport more than banning the technology outright or ignoring the disparity. (5) 'Leads players to act with integrity' — mandatory disclosure requirements support transparency and honest competition regardless of division. Marking points: (1) proposes a specific, workable strategy; (2) explicitly addresses each of the five framework evaluation criteria; (3) justification for each criterion is specific to the strategy, not generic; (4) strategy balances competing stakeholder interests; (5) includes a review/reflection element; (6) reasoning is grounded in the scenario's specific equipment/access issue; (7) demonstrates understanding that proportional treatment can differ from identical treatment." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A community sporting association debates whether to permanently ban an athlete found guilty of match-fixing, or offer a pathway to reintegration after a period of suspension and community service. Justify a position using the ethical decision-making framework's evaluation criteria. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: A time-limited suspension with structured reintegration better satisfies the framework's evaluation criteria overall. Against 'most good, least harm' — a permanent ban removes any possibility of the athlete demonstrating rehabilitated integrity, while a reintegration pathway allows harm to be addressed without foreclosing future good. Against 'respects the rights of all stakeholders' — this considers not only competitors harmed by the fixing but also the athlete's own stake in genuine rehabilitation, and the community's stake in the deterrent value of visible consequences alongside a credible path to redemption. Against 'serves the community as a whole' — a purely punitive permanent ban may satisfy an immediate desire for punishment but does less to rebuild long-term community confidence than a transparent, monitored reintegration process. Against 'leads players to act with integrity' — a visible reintegration pathway, conditional on real accountability, signals to other athletes that integrity violations have serious but not irreversible consequences, which may more effectively deter future breaches than a permanent ban alone. Judgement: reintegration with meaningful consequences better satisfies the framework's criteria than an outright permanent ban, though it depends on genuine accountability measures being enforced. Marking points: (1) states a clear position; (2) applies at least three of the framework's specific evaluation criteria; (3) explicitly weighs stakeholder interests against each other; (4) considers the deterrent versus rehabilitative functions of each option; (5) reaches a qualified, reasoned conclusion; (6) reasoning is grounded in the scenario." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A national sporting body's own primary data shows athletes trust its anti-corruption processes less after a widely publicised scandal, despite the body's secondary data showing corruption incidents have actually decreased since new policies were introduced. Analyse this apparent contradiction and justify what it implies for future ethics strategy design. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: The apparent contradiction is not actually contradictory — trust and actual incident rates are different measures, and a single, highly publicised scandal can disproportionately damage stakeholder trust (a perception-based, primary data measure) even while the underlying rate of incidents (a broader, secondary data measure) has genuinely improved, because high-profile events carry outsized psychological and reputational weight regardless of overall statistical trends. This suggests an ethics strategy focused only on reducing incident rates is necessary but not sufficient — because 'best serves the community as a whole' and maintaining community confidence depend on perceived trustworthiness, not just measured outcomes. Implication for strategy design: future ethics strategies should explicitly incorporate transparency and communication measures alongside the substantive anti-corruption policies already reducing incidents, since primary (trust) and secondary (incident rate) data reveal that outcomes and perception must both be actively managed. Marking points: (1) explains why the two data findings are not logically contradictory; (2) distinguishes trust/perception from incident rate as different measures; (3) connects the finding to the definition of integrity/community confidence; (4) draws a specific, justified implication for future strategy design; (5) reasoning uses both data sources as described in the scenario; (6) response is analytically grounded, not merely descriptive of the contradiction." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "An international sporting federation is criticised for inconsistent enforcement of gender-eligibility rules across different countries. Evaluate whether globalisation is the primary cause of this inconsistency, or whether it merely exposes inconsistencies that already existed. (6 marks)", back: "Model response: Globalisation is better understood as exposing and amplifying pre-existing inconsistencies rather than being their primary cause. Differing gender-eligibility rules and their enforcement likely stem from underlying differences in national sporting cultures, medical/legal frameworks, and values around gender that predate any specific globalised competition structure. However, globalisation genuinely intensifies the ethical consequences of these pre-existing inconsistencies: as competition and media coverage become interconnected across countries, athletes competing under differently enforced rules are now compared directly against one another on an international stage, and the disparity becomes publicly visible and consequential in a way it would not be if competition remained nationally siloed. In this sense globalisation is not the root cause, but it is a genuine causal factor in transforming a latent inconsistency into an active, high-stakes ethical dilemma requiring international resolution. Judgement: globalisation is not the primary cause but is a significant amplifying factor that turns a previously containable inconsistency into an urgent, internationally visible ethical issue. Marking points: (1) states a clear judgement (amplifier, not primary cause); (2) explains the likely underlying source of the inconsistency; (3) explains the specific mechanism by which globalisation intensifies the dilemma; (4) distinguishes 'cause' from 'amplifying/exposing factor' clearly; (5) reaches a nuanced, well-supported conclusion; (6) uses the concept of globalisation accurately and specifically." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A school PE class is designing its own code of conduct for intramural sport. One student argues unwritten rules (like respecting an injured opponent) don't need to be included because 'everyone already knows them'. Justify whether unwritten ethical expectations should be formally written into the code. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: Unwritten ethical expectations should be formally written into the code, because a code of behaviour functions to support ethical behaviour and fair play precisely by making expectations explicit and shared — assuming 'everyone already knows them' relies on informal transmission of values that is not guaranteed to be consistent across all participants, particularly newer or younger students who haven't yet absorbed the informal culture. Writing unwritten rules into the code also gives them the same standing as other conduct expectations when disputes arise, rather than leaving them dependent on subjective, after-the-fact agreement about what 'everyone' supposedly already understood. There is a valid counterpoint that overly codifying every unwritten norm risks making the code unwieldy and can strip some values of their voluntary, community-driven character if they become just another rule to follow. Judgement: core unwritten expectations directly related to fair play and safety should be written in, since the cost of one participant not sharing that assumption is high, while less critical social norms can remain informal. Marking points: (1) states a clear position; (2) explains why relying on informal transmission is risky/inconsistent; (3) explains the practical benefit of codification; (4) acknowledges a genuine counterpoint about over-codification; (5) reaches a qualified, reasoned conclusion; (6) response is grounded in the scenario." },
];

